{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1782558845,"nonce":"ZMSrPV9d87YAcif/4NXxvhcSmq0=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABG5p-Ow","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/339","origin":"github"},"title":"mru_transform: SBG as primary nav source, FCU as backup (decouple attitude from FCU gyro; mind altitude datum)","message":"## Motivation\n\nThe FCU 3D-gyro fault (#337) corrupted the FCU's attitude estimate — on June 26 `mavros/imu/data` showed phantom roll swings of +2.5°→+23.6° while the independent **SBG INS held the true hull roll steady at +4.4°** (the two agreed to 0.2° on the healthy June 18). Because `mru_transform` sources its orientation from `mavros/imu/data`, that phantom roll propagates into the `base_link` TF and gets painted into the bathymetry by `detections_to_pointcloud` — the roll artifacts seen in recent surveys.\n\nThe SBG stayed rock-solid through the gyro fault. **Make the SBG the primary navigation source and the FCU the backup**, so a degraded FCU can no longer poison bathy *or* nav attitude.\n\n## Current state\n\n`bizzyboat.yaml` (`/**/mru_transform`):\n```yaml\nsensors:\n  mru:\n    topics:\n      orientation: \"mavros/imu/data\"\n      position:    \"mavros/global_position/global\"\n      velocity:    \"mavros/local_position/velocity_body\"\nsensor_names: [mru]\n```\nSingle source, no failover.\n\n## mru_transform already supports priority + timeout failover\n\n`NavigationSensors::updateLatest` (navigation_sensors.hpp) scans `sensor_names` **in priority order** and adopts the highest-priority source whose latest sample is fresher than `sensor_timeout`, falling through to the next when one goes stale. It also publishes `nav/active_sensor/\u003cstream\u003e` so the live source is observable. So the mechanism is: order `[sbg, mru]` and set a sensible `sensor_timeout`.\n\n**Nuance:** arbitration is \"newest fresh sample wins, priority breaks ties,\" not strict primary-preference. With *both* sources healthy and at similar rates it can interleave SBG and FCU samples (which have different biases — we measured a fixed 1.9° attitude offset between them). Decide whether we need strict \"primary while fresh, backup only on dropout\" semantics (may need a small change), or whether running SBG at a higher rate so it's always the newest is acceptable.\n\n## Two blockers to resolve\n\n### 1. Message-type compatibility\n`mru_transform` consumes only `sensor_msgs/Imu` (orientation), `sensor_msgs/NavSatFix` or `geographic_msgs/GeoPose[Point]Stamped` (position), and `geometry_msgs/TwistStamped` (velocity). The SBG driver publishes `SbgEkfQuat` / `SbgEkfNav` / `SbgImuData`, none of which match. Need either the sbg_driver's standard-ROS-message outputs (it can emit `imu/data` as `sensor_msgs/Imu`) or small converter shims.\n\n### 2. ⚠️ Altitude datum mismatch (verified — must fix before switching POSITION)\nThe vertical pipeline is **ellipsoid-referenced** (`datum_config.hpp`; `chart_datum` applies a geoid grid; `map_tide` z ≈ 48.8 m). Measured on a June 26 bag at Lake Massabesic:\n\n| Source | Altitude | Datum |\n|--------|---------:|-------|\n| FCU `global_position/global` (current) | 48.75 m | **ellipsoidal** ✓ |\n| SBG `ekf_nav` | 80.86 m (undulation −32.79) | **MSL/orthometric** |\n| SBG `gps_pos` | 81.69 m (undulation −32.79) | MSL/orthometric |\n\nThe FCU already provides **ellipsoidal** height (which is why the current bathy vertical is correct). The SBG provides **MSL** — a **~32.8 m** difference. Consequences:\n- Switching the position source to SBG **without conversion drops the bathy vertical by ~33 m.**\n- A mixed SBG-primary / FCU-backup position setup makes the vertical **jump ~33 m on every failover** — a latent field hazard.\n- `mru_transform`'s `PositionSensor::navSatFixCallback` uses `NavSatFix.altitude` verbatim with **no geoid correction** (NavSatFix is spec'd as ellipsoidal, so feeding SBG-MSL into it is non-compliant and wrong).\n\nConversion is available: SBG publishes `undulation`, and ellipsoidal = altitude + undulation = 80.86 + (−32.79) = 48.07 m ≈ the FCU's 48.75 m. Any SBG→NavSatFix shim **must** apply this so both sources are on the same (ellipsoidal) datum.\n\n## Proposed approach\n\n1. **Phase 1 (the roll-artifact fix, altitude-neutral): switch orientation to SBG.** Add an `sbg` orientation source (`sensor_msgs/Imu` from `ekf_quat` + `imu_data`, via sbg_driver standard output or a shim), rank `[sbg, mru]`. Leave position on the FCU (already ellipsoidal) until Phase 2 is verified. This alone stops a bad FCU gyro from corrupting bathy/nav attitude.\n2. **Phase 2: SBG position/velocity as primary**, with an SBG→`NavSatFix` shim that converts MSL→ellipsoidal via `undulation`. Verify on bench + bag that SBG-vs-FCU vertical agree to \u003c1 m and that failover produces **no** vertical jump.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- With SBG primary, replaying a June 26 bag: the `base_link` attitude TF follows the SBG (steady ~4.4°), **not** the FCU phantom roll; re-projected bathy shows no roll artifact.\n- Failover verified: SBG dropout → FCU takes over with **no** attitude discontinuity and **no** vertical jump (datums matched).\n- `nav/active_sensor/*` reflects the live source.\n- Vertical datum unchanged vs today (ellipsoidal; `map_tide` z ≈ 48.8 m) — confirmed on a bag.\n\n## Related\n\n- #337 — FCU 3D-gyro RCA (the fault this hardens against).\n- #338 — time-sync diagnostics (the M3 +6 s, a separate fault from the same investigation).\n- #76 — M3/SBG/SVS payload integration on mercat (SBG availability/host).\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context)`","files":null}]}